PLACES=[
N('hawkwood-anchor-diamond-park','Hawkwood mansion ruins, Anchor Diamond Park','Ballston','sar','inst','ruins',42.93859,-73.87996,'area','public','walk','','1790s–1960s',
  'Henry Walton built a mansion here in the 1790s, choosing the Ballston countryside over Saratoga Springs, where other rich men were building. Later owners enlarged it; the town\'s markers say it was at its height in the early 1900s, when Governor Theodore Roosevelt called on the Guy Baker family, and that the Delavan family, temperance leaders, had owned it before. It fell into disrepair by the 1940s and burned in the 1960s. A trust left by Frank Schidzick bought the 246 acres for the Town of Ballston in 2015, and Saratoga PLAN holds a conservation easement on it.',
  'Signed ruins along the trails: the stone foundation of the mansion with its front steps, the curve of the bay window traced in the foundation and loose brick from its fireplaces; the remains of the windmill that pumped water from a man-made pond to the house, which the marker says made it the first house in Ballston with indoor plumbing; foundations of barns, a servants\' house and outhouses; and, on the yellow trail about 1.5 miles in, a free-standing fireplace that the town\'s 2016 digs could not explain. The markers were put up by the town and the Rotary Club in 2018 and 2019. Not checked on the ground.',
  'An estate Teddy Roosevelt visited, now foundations, a windmill base and a lone fireplace in the woods.',
  'Owned and managed by the Town of Ballston, open year-round from sunrise to sunset (Saratoga PLAN\'s page). Lot at the corner of Route 50 and Middle Line Road (1–17 Middle Line Road); the white entrance trail leads to the mansion site. Walking, snowshoeing and skiing only: no bicycles, horses or motor vehicles, no fires; dogs leashed. Do not dig or take anything.',
  [PLAN,HM('140710','Anchor Diamond Park (welcome panel)'),HM('134003','Hawkwood Mansion (foundation)'),HM('284370','Hawkwood Mansion Windmill'),HM('202049','Hawkwood Mansion (outbuildings)'),HM('235387','Hawkwood Mansion (the fireplace)')],
  conf='mixed',th=[42.93952,-73.87488],thn='Anchor Diamond Park lot, Route 50 and Middle Line Road',brick='?'),
N('lester-park-lime-kiln','Hoyt lime kiln and quarry, Lester Park','Saratoga Springs','sar','industry','ruins',43.09223,-73.84807,'site','public','drive','','late 1800s or early 1900s',
  'The Hoyt family quarried limestone here and burned it in a kiln for farm lime, which the sandy soils of the area needed. Across the road, Lester Park keeps a ledge of the same Hoyt limestone with fossil stromatolites, the sea floor of about 490 million years ago; it was given to the state in 1914 in memory of William Lester and is an outdoor exhibit of the New York State Museum. The sources differ on the kiln\'s age: the museum\'s marker says the late 1800s, Atlas Obscura the early 1900s.',
  'A crumbling stone lime kiln, hard to see behind leaves in summer (the marker\'s 2026 photograph), and the abandoned quarry about 100 metres to its left, which Atlas Obscura says can be walked through. Across the road, the polished rock outcrop with the cabbage-like stromatolite rings. Not checked on the ground.',
  'A farm lime kiln beside a fossil sea floor, both on one country road.',
  'Lester Park is a public park run by the New York State Museum (Wikipedia), and HMdb\'s editor notes that the kiln and park are one State Museum outdoor exhibit (the markers are the State Education Department\'s), on Lester Park Road (also signed Petrified Sea Gardens Road) south of Middle Grove Road, at about 41 Lester Park Road. A small pull-off for the Lester Park site is across the road from the kiln. Hours not checked: the museum\'s Lester Park page did not open. Do not hammer or take the fossils.',
  [HM('309883','Abandoned Lime Kiln'),HM('131595','Lester Park'),W('Petrified Sea Gardens'),L('Atlas Obscura: Lester Park','https://atlasobscura.com/places/lester-park')],conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('granger-glass-factory-mount-pleasant','Granger glass factory and Mount Pleasant site','Greenfield','sar','industry','traces',43.15420,-73.96930,'area','public','walk','','1842–1867',
  'Oscar Granger and others built a glass works and a company village called Mount Pleasant on the mountain above Lake Desolation. The town\'s marker gives 1842 and says the factory closed 25 years later and the place slowly died; DEC\'s 2002 plan says Granger bought 1,400 acres and built a sawmill about 1842 and the glass works in 1844, with five furnaces, a plank road from near Saratoga by way of Porter Corners, over 20 houses, a store, school, church, post office, mills and a temperance hotel. The site is a designated state historic site (archaeological) on state forest land.',
  'DEC\'s plan describes the ground west of Lake Desolation Road: pits and mounds where bottle hunters have dug, waste glass, blue glass, brick and old cellar holes. A small cemetery was said to lie just south, though the plan\'s author found nothing of it on the ground. Unmarked trails lead in from the road; the exact spot of the furnaces is not pinned, and the pin is the town\'s marker by the forest access. Not checked on the ground.',
  'A glass-blowing village of the 1840s, now cellar holes and blue glass in a state forest.',
  'New York State land: Lake Desolation State Forest (DEC Region 5), open year-round, free. Park at the DEC lot on Lake Desolation Road (DEC\'s coordinate 43.15397, -73.96912); the glass factory is reached by an undesignated trail. Hunting is allowed in season: wear orange in the fall. It is an archaeological site: do not dig or take glass. Private inholdings nearby: keep to state land.',
  [HM('136259','Glass Factory'),DEC,DECP],conf='mixed',th=[43.15397,-73.96912],thn='DEC lot on Lake Desolation Road',brick='?'),
N('rock-city-falls-west-mills','Rock City Falls: George West\'s mills and the falls','Milton','sar','industry','standing',43.06307,-73.91583,'area','view','drive','','1859–',
  'Saw and grist mills stood at the falls of the Kayaderosseras Creek before 1800, and paper mills followed in the mid-1800s. George West, the "Paper Bag King" who made his fortune on the folded paper bag, set up his Empire Mill here in 1862 (Wikipedia) and built the Excelsior Mill beside it in 1866; The Mansion of Saratoga\'s history page dates the Empire Mill to the early 1800s and calls it the first of the many mills West owned after the Civil War. From 1898 to 1929 an electric freight trolley, the Ballston Terminal Railroad, served the mills. Cottrell Paper still works in the hamlet.',
  'Beside the Route 29 bridge a stone mill building carries the inscription "Rock City Mills 1859" (nnywaterfalls.com), and the falls are a short walk back from the bridge. The Mansion\'s page calls the Empire Mill across from the West house deserted and says the trails behind it pass ruins of other mills and the old railroad. What stands of each mill in 2026 was not checked; Wikipedia says the Empire and Excelsior mills are part of a park.',
  'The Paper Bag King\'s first mills, by the falls that drove them.',
  'View from Route 29 and the bridge. The Town of Milton\'s Cottrell-Herrington Park (formerly Rock City Falls Park) is on Route 29 in the hamlet: a fishing spot with a short paved trail. Who owns the mill buildings was not checked: treat them as private and do not go inside. The pin is the town\'s hamlet marker by the Cottrell Paper entrance.',
  [W('Rock City Falls, New York'),HM('127292','Rock City Falls'),L('The Mansion of Saratoga: history','https://www.themansionsaratoga.com/history'),L('Northern New York Waterfalls: Rock City Falls','https://www.nnywaterfalls.com/kayaderosserascreek/rockcityfalls'),MILTON],conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('boice-park-pioneer-mill-trolley','Ballston Terminal trolley bed and Pioneer Mill dam, Boice Family Park','Milton','sar','rail','traces',43.04781,-73.93282,'area','public','walk','','1850–1929',
  'About a dozen mills and their ponds strung along the Kayaderosseras between Rock City Falls and Ballston Spa, and from 1896 to 1929 an electric trolley carried their raw materials, goods and workers. The Pioneer Mill was a paper mill by 1850; it burned in 1869 after a foreclosure dispute, George West bought the ruins in 1870 and rebuilt it to make paper for his bag factory, and it ran until the trolley stopped in 1929, when it was torn down (Friends of the Kayaderosseras).',
  'The trolley bed forms part of the trails, and the dents left by its ties can still be seen, with parts of the Pioneer Mill dam, according to Friends of the Kayaderosseras (a 2014 article). The park has about four miles of rugged, sometimes muddy trails beside the creek. The pin is the park as OpenStreetMap maps it, not the dam. Not checked on the ground.',
  'Walk a paper-mill trolley line through the woods by its tie marks.',
  'Town of Milton park, given by the Boice family: walking and hiking trails only. Public lots on Creekside Drive off Rock City Road in a housing development. No alcohol, no motor vehicles or ATVs; dogs leashed. Hours not checked.',
  [L('Friends of the Kayaderosseras: Gravity made it happen','https://kayadeross.org/2014/05/12/gravity-made-it-happen/'),L('Friends of the Kayaderosseras: Boice Family Park','https://kayadeross.org/park/boice-family-park/'),MILTON],conf='mixed',th=[43.04862,-73.92867],thn='Boice Family Park lot, Creekside Drive',brick='?'),
N('george-west-house-rock-city-falls','George West House (The Mansion of Saratoga)','Rock City Falls','sar','landmark','standing',43.06663,-73.92776,'exact','view','drive','','1866',
  'George West, an English immigrant who became the world\'s largest maker of folded paper bags and a congressman from 1881 to 1889, built this 23-room Italian villa across from his first mill in 1866. The family lived here until 1875, when they moved to a larger house in Ballston Spa (since razed), and it became their summer home. It has since been a private home, an antique shop and a bed and breakfast, and is now an event venue. It was listed on the Register in 2005.',
  'A two-storey wood-frame house with a hipped roof, a full-height projecting entrance pavilion, an ornate flat-roofed portico and a hipped belvedere with finials in the middle of the roof (Wikipedia); a carriage barn and corncrib stand with it. The venue\'s page says the interior is little changed. Seen from Route 29; inside only at events.',
  'The Paper Bag King\'s villa, still facing his first mill.',
  'Private: an event venue at 801 Route 29. Look from the road; the inside is seen only by guests at events. Parking is the venue\'s own.',
  [W('George West House'),L('The Mansion of Saratoga: history','https://www.themansionsaratoga.com/history')],brick='?'),
N('charlton-hamlet-historic-district','Charlton hamlet and the Freehold church','Charlton','sar','town','standing',42.93417,-73.96556,'area','view','drive','','1787–',
  'Settlers from Freehold, Monmouth County, New Jersey organised a Presbyterian church here in 1788, and the hamlet grew round a crossroads; boundary markers name families settled from 1774 on. The Register district (1976) covers 123 acres and 37 buildings dating from 1787: the Greek Revival Freehold Presbyterian Church of 1852–53, the Charlton House tavern of about 1791, the village shop of about 1829, a stucco schoolhouse of 1859 and the former St. Paul\'s Episcopal Church, built in 1804, now the historical society\'s museum. Charlton Academy, founded in 1859, burned in April 1948; its marker stands before the church.',
  'A white crossroads hamlet: almost every building is wood painted white (Wikipedia), the big columned Presbyterian church the largest. The Charlton Historical Society opens the old St. Paul\'s church at 2009 Maple Avenue as its museum on summer weekend afternoons. Interiors not checked.',
  'A New Jersey congregation\'s hamlet, kept white and wooden since the 1790s.',
  'Public roads; the houses are private. The museum at 2009 Maple Avenue is open Saturday and Sunday 2 to 4 pm from July 11 to August 30 and on Founder\'s Day weekend (June 6 and 7 in 2026), and by appointment (the society\'s page). The church is an active congregation. Parking not checked.',
  [W('Charlton Historic District'),HM('126733','Presbyterian Church'),HM('126743','Charlton Museum'),HM('126385','Charlton Academy'),L('Charlton Historical Society: museum','https://charltonnyhs.org/museum.html')],brick='?'),
N('west-charlton-scotch-church','West Charlton United Presbyterian Church ("Scotch Church")','West Charlton','sar','worship','standing',42.97278,-74.03056,'exact','view','drive','','1880–81; burial ground 1778',
  'Scottish settlers founded the congregation in 1792, and its burying ground is older: the marker gives 1778. The present church was built about 1880 (Wikipedia) and dedicated in 1881 (the marker); a polygonal chapel was added in 1904. It was listed on the Register in 1998.',
  'A late-Victorian wood-frame church with engaged corner towers; the north tower carries a louvred belfry under a tall spire. The cemetery is beside it. Interior not checked.',
  'A Scots congregation of 1792 under a Carpenter Gothic spire.',
  'At 1331 Sacandaga Road (Route 147) at Route 67. A church: look from the road, and walk the cemetery with respect. Service times not checked.',
  [W('West Charlton United Presbyterian Church'),HM('62717','Scotch Church')],brick='no'),
N('calvary-episcopal-burnt-hills','Calvary Episcopal Church, Burnt Hills','Ballston','sar','worship','standing',42.90987,-73.89156,'exact','view','drive','','1849–58',
  'Founded by Dr. Edward Davis in May 1849, with its first service in the building on Christmas Eve 1849. The board-and-batten Carpenter Gothic church with its central tower was enlarged with transepts in 1858; an arsonist set it alight in 1966, and it was restored and put back in use between 1966 and 1978. The parish hall dates from 1877 and 1895, and the rectory across the road from 1856. It is on the Register.',
  'Steep roof, board-and-batten walls and simple Gothic trim on a knoll on Lake Hill Road, with a small cemetery behind. Inside, the Register form records plank floors, slip pews, some original and later stained glass and a memorial plaque by Isabel Moore Kimball. The interior is seen at services.',
  'An 1849 wooden Gothic church that came back from an arson fire.',
  'Active parish at 85 Lake Hill Road, Burnt Hills. Look from the road; service times not checked.',
  [NR('100000498','Calvary Episcopal Church'),HM('92737','Calvary Episcopal Church')],brick='?'),
N('packer-farm-barkersville-store','Packer farm, Barkersville store and Packers Pond dam','Providence','sar','landmark','standing',43.08891,-74.03855,'area','view','drive','','c. 1825–1893',
  'Barkersville, first called Barker\'s Hollow, was settled in 1796 by David and Samuel Barker, who built water-powered saw and grist mills, a tannery and a shoe shop (the town marker). The Packer house, of about 1825 with Greek Revival changes, was moved to this site about 1857; the English barn is of about 1825, a second barn of about 1860 and the frame store of about 1893. The concrete dam holding Packers Pond, an old mill pond on Alder Creek, is part of the Register listing (2013).',
  'From Barkersville Road: the two-storey hip-roofed farmhouse, two frame barns, the small frame store and the dam with its pond across the road. The Register form says the house keeps its plan and much original woodwork and the store its shelving. Interiors not checked; it is a private farm.',
  'A mill-hamlet crossroads: farmhouse, barns, a country store and the mill pond.',
  'Private, at 7189 Barkersville Road (County Route 16). Look from the road only.',
  [W('Packer Farm and Barkersville Store'),NR('13000630','Packer Farm and Barkersville Store'),HM('131132','Barkersville')],brick='?'),
N('copeland-carriage-shop-beecher-hollow','Copeland Carriage Shop and mill dam, Beecher Hollow','Edinburg','sar','industry','standing',43.22100,-74.10040,'area','view','drive','','about 1830',
  'A rare small rural workshop, built about 1830 for the brothers Arad and Leonard Copeland, probably as an addition to a carding mill of 1817 that Eli Beecher had put up on Beecher Creek. It made carriages and wagons, later served as Latcher\'s machine shop until 1916, and has been empty since. The Edinburg Historical Society bought it in 2005 and has stabilised it; it is on the Register.',
  'A story-and-a-half timber-framed shop with a classical frame round its gable front and a Palladian-style window motif, double doors at ground and loft level and a hoist at the ridge. Behind it is the breached remnant of the stone dam that powered it, and to the east the foundation of the lost carding-mill block; below is the 20-foot Copeland Falls. The 1879 Copeland covered bridge is just south. Interior not checked.',
  'A wagon shop of about 1830 beside its broken dam and a waterfall.',
  'Owned by the Edinburg Historical Society, on North Shore Road by the Copeland covered bridge. Look from the road; whether it is ever opened was not checked. The pin is set from the covered bridge\'s position and the form\'s description, not from a mapped point.',
  [NR('100002513','Copeland Carriage Shop')],conf='mixed',brick='?',apart='copeland-covered-bridge: the carriage shop stands beside the covered bridge; two structures'),
N('barker-general-store-beecher-hollow','Barker General Store, Beecher Hollow','Edinburg','sar','civic','standing',43.22167,-74.10444,'site','view','drive','','1847, 1870',
  'A country store built in 1847, with a two-storey shed-roofed addition of 1870, in a style between Federal and Greek Revival. A concealed space inside is believed to have hidden people escaping slavery on the Underground Railroad (Wikipedia, which gives no source for it). It was listed on the Register in 2002.',
  'A tall portico on four turned Tuscan columns across the front of the gable-roofed store. Present use and interior not checked.',
  'A hamlet store with a hidden room, by the last covered bridge in the county.',
  'Beecher Hollow, Edinburg, on the road by the Copeland covered bridge. Owner and present use not checked: look from the road.',
  [W('Barker General Store')],conf='mixed',brick='some'),
N('batchellerville-presbyterian-church','Batchellerville Presbyterian Church','Edinburg','sar','worship','standing',43.20918,-74.08155,'site','view','drive','','1867; moved 1930–31',
  'A Greek Revival timber-framed church built in 1867 in Batchellerville. When the Conklingville dam made the Sacandaga Reservoir (now Great Sacandaga Lake), which drowned ten communities in whole or part, the church was moved up to this site to escape the water: 1930 says the town marker, 1931 Wikipedia. It was listed on the Register in 2000.',
  'A plain rectangular church with a two-stage louvred belfry under a bell-cast metal roof, near the east end of the Batchellerville Bridge, with the town\'s Great Sacandaga marker across the road. Interior not checked.',
  'A church that was carried uphill ahead of the reservoir.',
  'On South Shore Road (County Route 7) near Degolia Road. Look from the road; whether it holds services was not checked.',
  [W('Batchellerville Presbyterian Church'),HM('122295','Batchellerville Presbyterian Church'),HM('131535','Great Sacandaga Lake')],conf='mixed',brick='?',apart='batchellerville: the drowned hamlet as a whole; this is its church, which still stands'),
N('smiths-feed-store-elnora','Smith\'s Grain and Feed Store, Elnora','Clifton Park','sar','civic','standing',42.89317,-73.82124,'site','view','drive','','1892',
  'George Smith ran a coal yard and grain business at the Elnora railroad crossing; the hamlet was named in 1882 after the wife of a Delaware and Hudson official (the town marker). The store was built in 1892 and was listed on the Register in 2012.',
  'A two-and-a-half-storey clapboard building with three additions, a standing-seam metal roof and a ventilator cupola, and a smaller early-1900s railroad building beside it (Wikipedia). Present use and interior not checked.',
  'A coal and feed store at a railroad crossing, cupola and all.',
  'At the Elnora railroad crossing, Clifton Park. Owner and present use not checked: look from the road, and keep off the railroad.',
  [W('Smith\'s Grain and Feed Store'),HM('129003','Store 1892')],conf='mixed',brick='some'),
N('jonesville-store','Jonesville Store and Jonesville Cemetery','Clifton Park','sar','civic','standing',42.91014,-73.82340,'site','open','drive','','about 1860; cemetery 1864',
  'A general store built around 1860 and enlarged about 1900, which also held Jonesville\'s post office; its 2020 owner told the Saratoga Business Journal it probably began in the 1850s, when the hamlet was a farm community. It was listed on the Register in 2011. The rural cemetery nearby was laid out in 1864 by Burton A. Thomas and holds 252 people moved there early on, with burials from 1799 to 1863; it is still in use and was listed in 2014.',
  'A clapboard store with a gable roof, a full-width front porch and a two-storey rear block, on a foundation of brick over limestone. Since about 2020 it has been a restaurant (Classic Crust, per the Saratoga Business Journal); the Register list calls it vacant. Interior not checked.',
  'A Civil War-era crossroads store, still where Jonesville eats.',
  'Private business at 989 Main Street (Route 146) at Longkill Road. Look from the road or go in as a customer when it is open (hours not checked). The cemetery is an active burial ground: visit with respect.',
  [W('Jonesville Store'),W('Jonesville Cemetery'),L('Saratoga Business Journal (2020): former Jonesville Store will become a restaurant','https://saratoga.com/saratogabusinessjournal/2020/02/former-jonesville-store-in-clifton-park-will-become-a-wood-fired-pizza-restaurant'),NR('14000132','Jonesville Cemetery')],conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('grooms-tavern-clifton-park','Grooms Tavern and the Mohawk Valley Grange Hall','Clifton Park','sar','hotel','standing',42.83487,-73.84408,'exact','open','drive','','about 1825; 1840s; 1896',
  'A tavern of about 1825, raised to two storeys in the 1840s, that also served as a general store; the town\'s first board meeting was held here on April 1, 1828. Behind it stand a wagon and blacksmith shop of the 1840s and a privy. Long known as Klingbeil\'s store, it stood empty for over a decade before the Town of Clifton Park bought it in 1999, and reopened as a historical and cultural centre in April 2007. The Grange hall nearby was built in 1896, bought by the Grange in 1931 and given to the town in 2004. Both are on the Register.',
  'The two-storey Federal tavern with its Greek Revival cornice, the Greek Revival wagon and blacksmith shop, the restored outhouse, and inside a headstone found during the rebuilding (Lakes to Locks Passage). The Grange hall is a one-and-a-half-storey timber frame on a dry-laid stone foundation, a short way south.',
  'A crossroads tavern where the town first met, now the town\'s history centre.',
  'Town of Clifton Park, at 290 Sugar Hill Road (Grooms Road), Rexford, with a lot beside it. Free; the building opens for scheduled tours and programs (Lakes to Locks Passage); hours not checked. Booking through the town\'s parks office.',
  [W('Grooms Tavern Complex'),W('Mohawk Valley Grange Hall'),L('Lakes to Locks Passage: Historic Grooms Tavern','https://passageport.org/places-to-go/historic-grooms-tavern/'),L('Town of Clifton Park: land by Grooms Tavern and the Grange acquired','https://cliftonparkny.gov/clifton-park-land-acquisition-property-bordering-grooms-tavern-and-the-grange-acquired')],th=[42.83468,-73.84482],thn='Lot behind Grooms Tavern (OpenStreetMap)',brick='?'),
]
